                <h2>A Weekly Review Uncovers Three Hidden Issues</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team advertised across Meta, TikTok, and Google Ads. On Monday morning, the team was preparing to increase budgets but noticed that spending velocity had become unusually high on several campaigns. Instead of immediately raising the budgets, the account operator followed the company’s new compliance checklist and reviewed each area.</p>
<p>The review identified three issues: a payment-information update had not been confirmed internally; a set of video ads used a strong savings claim without retaining the supporting test records; and the landing pages still displayed old prices that did not match the promotional content in the ads. Although these issues belonged to account, creative, and landing-page operations, treating them separately could have left serious fund, account, and consumer-protection risks unresolved.</p>
<p>The following SOP provides a foundation for weekly reviews and pre-campaign compliance checks.</p>
<h2>A Compliance Review Covers Four Layers</h2>
<h3>1. Account Layer: Identity, Access, and Payments</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check for restrictions, review failures, failed payments, identity-verification requests, and unusual account notices.</li>
<li>Confirm that the administrator email, legal entity, payment method, agency access, and internal owner match the approved account setup. Remove access held by former employees.</li>
<li>Verify recharge amounts, currencies, paying entities, and invoice details. Flag unusually large or repeated recharges for additional approval.</li>
<li>Organize multiple brands, regions, and account purposes separately. Review shared payment arrangements that could create unintended account associations.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Creative Layer: Every Claim Must Be Supportable</h3>
<ul>
<li>Archive the original files, scripts, copy, cover images, and final live versions. A platform thumbnail is not sufficient evidence.</li>
<li>Link discount, free-trial, speed, performance, ranking, and comparison claims to supporting records such as pricing tests, terms of service, or internal approvals.</li>
<li>Pay particular attention to regulated categories such as health, finance, gambling, and education, as well as youth targeting, sensitive political content, and special advertising restrictions.</li>
<li>Review user comments for complaints that conflict with the ad promise. Determine whether the creative or landing page requires an update.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Landing Page and Tracking Layer: Consistent Information and Attribution</h3>
<ul>
<li>Compare the headline, visuals, calls to action, and promotional conditions with the landing page. Check for outdated prices, unavailable inventory, or undisclosed charges.</li>
<li>Confirm that every data field collected by the lead form is necessary for the stated business purpose.</li>
<li>Verify that UTMs, landing pages, conversion events, and authorized domains correspond correctly to prevent tracking loss or attribution errors.</li>
<li>Test the entire journey on mobile devices, including geo redirects, broken links, and loading speed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Evidence Layer: Document Every Decision</h3>
<p>The audit log should include the platform, account, creative ID, issue description, risk level, owner, deadline, action taken, and verification result. Screenshots must show the date and full context. Store pre-change and post-change files separately. Questions involving local laws or regulated industries should be reviewed by legal counsel or a qualified external adviser rather than resolved solely through operational judgment.</p>
<h2>Triage Issues Instead of Stopping Every Campaign</h2>
<p>A practical framework uses three levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>P0 – Critical:</strong> Suspected fraud, altered payment information, account restrictions, violations in sensitive categories, or potentially unlawful claims. Pause relevant operations immediately, restrict access, and notify the responsible executive and compliance adviser.</li>
<li><strong>P1 – High priority:</strong> Mismatched landing-page information, missing evidence, expired permissions, or tracking errors. Resolve these before the next campaign cycle and validate revised ads with a limited budget.</li>
<li><strong>P2 – Routine optimization:</strong> Naming inconsistencies, incomplete screenshots, or weak filing labels. Assign these to normal maintenance with clear owners and deadlines.</li>
</ul>
<p>Risk should be assessed across four dimensions: potential financial loss, account security, consumer impact, and the number of affected creatives or accounts. An issue involving substantial spend, a sensitive category, or several markets should automatically receive a higher priority.</p>
<h2>A Six-Step Workflow for Action and Verification</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify and log:</strong> Assign every issue a unique reference and avoid managing it only through chat messages.</li>
<li><strong>Contain the risk:</strong> Pause affected ads, charges, or permission changes when necessary, but do not delete the evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm the rule source:</strong> Record the platform notice, policy link, and applicable version of the internal procedure.</li>
<li><strong>Assign ownership:</strong> Send account matters to account operations, creative issues to the creative and compliance teams, and landing-page issues to the website or e-commerce team.</li>
<li><strong>Remediate and document:</strong> Preserve the reason, versions, screenshots, and approval record for every modification.</li>
<li><strong>Verify before resuming:</strong> Have someone other than the person who made the correction confirm the fix. If verification fails, reassess the severity and escalate the case.</li>
</ol>
<h2>The Outcome: Measure Risk Reduction, Not Just Completed Checklists</h2>
<p>In this case, the team paused the affected creatives rather than increasing the budget. The account owner confirmed the source of the payment update and completed the internal approval process. The creative team attached the promotional test records, while the e-commerce team updated the outdated pricing pages. After verification, the campaigns were relaunched with limited budgets and did not generate similar warnings during the following three days.</p>
<p>The response prevented a potential chargeback and prevented the team from scaling a non-compliant campaign. More importantly, the team converted the findings into new creative-review rules, turning compliance from reactive troubleshooting into a traceable operating process. Companies should perform a standard review each week and add targeted checks before major promotions, product launches, landing-page changes, and changes to account ownership. Leaders should also test whether each owner can complete the required actions within the assigned deadline.</p>
